for these products have been 'Chicago and New York, which set the prices for this continent. Even while the government was negotiating its treaty with the United States the agricultural bloc at Washington were framing the Bailey amendment to the 1936 United States revenue bill, imposing a duty of 3 cents per pound against produce of Canada which had been going into that market for the last sixty years, and thereby cutting it off over-night. What can be done about it? Nothing? While that was going on in the United States our fellow citizens in the empire have been busily producing, in Nigeria, the Straits Settlements and Ceylon, millions and millions of pounds of vegetable oils and shipping them into this country free of any duty and any tax. In 1933 the amount was 119,000,000 pounds; by 1936 it had risen to 224,000,000 pounds. The United States has imposed taxation of five cents per pound on cocoanut oil, four cents per pound on palm kernel oil, and three cents per pound on palm oil. Their action, which was taken in June of last year, tended to back up into the world's markets a, billion pounds of vegetable oils. England is terribly worried about it. The Minister of Finance when he was in England was worried about it, and the world at large is concerned about it from this general angle, that the vegetable kingdom is fast replacing the animal kingdom. If the situation has caused anxiety in the world generally, we in Canada, which is an animal producing country dependent more on animal than on vegetable products, have cause to be doubly concerned and promptly to bestir ourselves. What is happening is that the product of these vegetable oils is replacing the production that these
46,000	boys and many more of whom the minister has spoken are trying to dispose of, and it is doing so by leaps and bounds. Today we have a record surplus of butter;
25,000,000 pounds of butter in Canada requires a market. I am pleased to see that the Prime Minister has come in, because I want him to get the full significance of this.
